WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.697
This is a clock face. It has
two hands, and they move.

2
00:00:07.917 --> 00:00:11.632
Today you will read the time
on it, down to five minutes.

3
00:00:12.132 --> 00:00:15.801
The short fat hand shows the
hour. Read that one first.

4
00:00:16.021 --> 00:00:19.969
The long thin hand shows the
minutes. Read that one next.

5
00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:23.904
Here the short hand is at the
3, so the hour is three o'clock.

6
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:27.885
Watch the long hand travel once
round. That trip is one hour.

7
00:00:28.105 --> 00:00:32.331
The short hand crept along too.
It moved from the 3 to the 4.

8
00:00:32.831 --> 00:00:36.314
The clock face splits into
four quarters, like a cake.

9
00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:39.925
A quarter of the way round, at
the 3, is quarter past three.

10
00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:43.860
Half way round, straight down
at the 6, is half past three.

11
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.074
Three quarters round, at the
9, is quarter to the next hour.

12
00:00:48.294 --> 00:00:51.916
Now look at the short hand. It
is nearly all the way to the 4.

13
00:00:52.136 --> 00:00:55.387
So this is quarter to
four, not quarter to three.

14
00:00:55.887 --> 00:01:00.113
The long hand points at the 4.
Count round from the 12 in fives.

15
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:04.094
Five, ten, fifteen, twenty. It
is twenty minutes past three.

16
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:07.797
It is not four minutes past.
Every number counts five minutes.

17
00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:11.918
On the right side it is past.
On the left side it is to.

18
00:01:12.418 --> 00:01:17.945
A digital clock writes the time in
numbers, hour first, minutes after.

19
00:01:18.165 --> 00:01:21.740
Half past two is written two
thirty, with a colon between.

20
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.326
Quarter past seven is written
seven fifteen on a digital clock.

21
00:01:26.546 --> 00:01:31.515
Quarter to five is written four
forty five. The hour is still four.

22
00:01:32.015 --> 00:01:35.730
Time has its own units, from
very short to very long.

23
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.176
Sixty seconds make one minute.
Sixty minutes make one hour.

24
00:01:40.396 --> 00:01:44.344
Twenty four hours make one
day. Seven days make one week.

25
00:01:44.564 --> 00:01:48.557
Twelve months make one year.
One blink lasts about a second.

26
00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:52.773
A calendar lays one whole month
out in rows of seven days.

27
00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:56.383
Each row is one week, running
from Monday to Sunday.

28
00:01:56.603 --> 00:02:00.829
The twelfth of June sits under
Friday. Read up the column.

29
00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:04.626
Short hand for the hour, and
long hand for the minutes.

30
00:02:04.846 --> 00:02:09.304
Quarter past at the 3, and quarter
to at the 9. Count in fives.

31
00:02:09.524 --> 00:02:13.890
Write it in numbers, read a calendar,
and know your units of time.
